            'body_en' => "The Romans called the city Amida and made it the great fortress of their eastern frontier against Persia. The walls as they stand were raised in their present line under the emperor Constantius II around 349 — just in time to hold out, in 359, for seventy-three days against the Sasanian king Shapur II before the city fell and was emptied, a siege described by an officer who was inside, Ammianus Marcellinus. Justinian repaired them in the sixth century; the Arabs took the city in 639.\n\nEvery ruler after that built, and signed the work. The Abbasid caliph al-Mu\'tadid had stretches pulled down in 899 to punish a rebellious governor; his successor rebuilt the gates in 909, and from then on the towers carry Kufic inscriptions and carved animals — the Marwanid Kurdish emirs of the eleventh century, the Seljuks after 1088, and above all the Artuqids, whose two great bastions of 1208 are among the finest medieval military architecture anywhere.\n\nThe Ottomans took the city in 1515 and the walls lost their purpose, though not their presence. A breach was cut for traffic in 1930; houses were built against the stone; and between 2000 and 2007 the city cleared them, cleaned the basalt and laid a park along the foot. In 2015 the walls and the Hevsel Gardens below them became a World Heritage Site — months before fighting inside the old city destroyed much of what they enclosed.",
